<section class="principles section" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">The direction</p><h2 id="principles-title">More control.<br>More room to choose.</h2><p>The aim is to keep your data and identity yours, make providers replaceable, and give you a way back when something fails.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="principle-list">
    <article>
      <span class="index">01</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Private</h3><p>You decide who can access your data and what they can do with it. Privacy begins with control and clear boundaries; it is never an absolute security guarantee.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="index">02</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Decentralized</h3><p>Ownership belongs across individuals and businesses. Providers should be replaceable, so changing a service does not mean surrendering your identity or starting over.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="index">03</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Open-source</h3><p>Software you can inspect and adapt offers a path to continuity beyond one vendor. The goal is to keep the tools useful—and maintainable—on your terms.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="index">04</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Agent-first</h3><p>Design for useful agent assistance from the start: explicit permissions, understandable actions, and a person in control. Reliable assistance has to be demonstrated, not assumed.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
